✅ A variable is a named memory location used to store data that can change during program execution.
    int age = 20;
    
    int → Data Type
    age → Variable Name
    20 → Value

✅ Variable declaration is the process of creating a variable by specifying its data type and name.
    data_type variable_name;

✅ Variable initialization is the process of assigning an initial value to a variable.
    data_type variable_name = value;

    #include <stdio.h>
    int main()
    {
        int marks = 80;
        printf("Marks = %d\n", marks);
        marks = 95;                     => Changing Variable Value
        printf("Updated Marks = %d", marks);
        return 0;
    }

Multiple Variable Declaration => int a, b, c;
Multiple Variable Initialization => int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

Variable Naming Rules
----------------------
✅ Start with a letter or _
✅ Digits allowed after the first character
✅ No spaces
✅ No special symbols (except _)
✅ Cannot use keywords

    #include <stdio.h>
    int main()
    {
        int age = 21;
        float height = 5.8;
        char grade = 'A';
        printf("Age = %d\n", age);
        printf("Height = %.1f\n", height);
        printf("Grade = %c\n", grade);
        return 0;
    }

Summary
-------
✅ A variable is a named memory location used to store data.
✅ The value of a variable can change during program execution.
✅ Every variable has a data type, name, and optionally an initial value.
✅ Declaration creates a variable, while initialization assigns its first value.
✅ Variables should have meaningful names and follow C naming rules.
✅ Choosing the correct data type is important to store data accurately.
